{{#each results}}
The organisation knows which authorities it must contact, when, and how. It keeps the contact details up to date, so that it can report in time and answer the requests of authorities.

== How to decide if this control is required

This control is always required. Every organisation must be able to contact authorities, for example the data protection authority about a personal data breach, or the police about a crime.

== How to implement

. List the authorities that the organisation must or can contact. For example: the national CSIRT, the competent authority for NIS2, the data protection authority, the market surveillance authorities of the products, and the police.
. For each authority, record when to contact it, the reporting channel or the contact details, and who in the organisation contacts it.
. Record the reporting deadlines that apply. For example, the GDPR (Regulation (EU) 2016/679, Article 33) requires that a personal data breach is notified to the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours.
. Make sure that the people in the incident management process can find the list during an incident, also when the normal systems are not available.
. Check the contact details at least once a year.

